      "math_academy_id": "e-5118",
      "problem": "If $\\log_2 x = 5,$ then what is the value of $x?$",
      "worked_solution": "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2,$ so we use a base of $2$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡x & =5 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡x} & ={2}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $2^{\\log_2 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5} \\\\ x & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
